title: k8s_get output=yaml emits invalid YAML for every boolean field
updated: 2026-08-16T01:54:13Z
---

k8s_get with output => 'yaml' runs YAML::XS::Dump($obj->TO_JSON). TO_JSON returns JSON::PP::Boolean objects for every boolean field, and YAML::XS has no idea what those are, so it serialises the Perl guts:

    spec:
      containers:
      - securityContext:
          privileged: !!perl/scalar:JSON::PP::Boolean 0
          readOnlyRootFilesystem: !!perl/scalar:JSON::PP::Boolean 1
      hostNetwork: !!perl/scalar:JSON::PP::Boolean 1

Measured on a real IO::K8s Pod object, not a hand-rolled struct. Kubernetes resources are full of booleans (hostNetwork, privileged, readOnly, automountServiceAccountToken, allowPrivilegeEscalation, ...), so this hits routinely rather than in a corner case.

Two consequences: the output is not a valid manifest — kubectl apply rejects it — and the LLM is shown Perl internals where it expects YAML, which is exactly the kind of thing that makes a model invent a workaround.

Fix: IO::K8s already ships the correct serialiser. Role::APIObject::to_yaml does

    YAML::PP->new(schema => [qw/JSON/], boolean => 'JSON::PP')->dump_string($self->TO_JSON)

which produces 'hostNetwork: true'. So k8s_get should call $obj->to_yaml instead of dumping TO_JSON itself.

This also settles the YAML::XS question: YAML::PP is a hard requires of IO::K8s (cpanfile line 6), so it is always present wherever MCP::K8s runs. The eval { require YAML::XS } guard and its silent JSON fallback can go entirely — no optional dependency, no 'recommends' entry needed, and no more silently degrading to JSON when the user asked for YAML.

Check while doing it: to_yaml lives in IO::K8s::Role::APIObject, so confirm the objects k8s_get holds actually compose that role (they should — it is the API object role) and decide what happens if one does not.
